        "Economic growth is a sustained rise in real output per head; development is broader, entailing structural change, wider choices, better health and education, and reduced poverty. Growth can proceed without development when gains concentrate in few hands or sectors, as oil booms showed. Measures range from GDP per capita through composite indices like the Human Development Index. In {S}, the growth-development distinction, with a Nigerian illustration of jobless growth, opens every serious essay.",
        "The Lewis model divides the economy into a traditional, subsistence sector with surplus labour at near-zero marginal product and a modern sector paying a wage above it, drawing workers away and accumulating profits for reinvestment until the surplus is exhausted, the Lewis turning point. It explains urban migration alongside rural underemployment. In {S}, applying the model to Nigeria means asking why the modern sector absorbed labour so slowly, capital intensity and weak manufacturing being the answers.",
        "Rostow's stages run from traditional society through preconditions for take-off, take-off driven by leading manufacturing sectors, the drive to maturity, to the age of high mass consumption. The stages fit some histories but are criticised as Eurocentric and linear. Structural change, labour moving from low-productivity farming to higher-productivity industry and services, remains the robust core idea. In {S}, use the model as a lens on Nigerian data, services expanding before industry, rather than reciting stages.",
        "The vicious circle of poverty argues low income causes low saving, hence low investment, hence low productivity, hence low income again, trapping poor economies and poor households alike; malnutrition lowers learning and earnings in the same loop. Breaking out needs an outside push: infrastructure, credit access, education and health investment. In {S}, the circle is a hypothesis about mechanisms, and citing Nigerian evidence, such as thin rural credit and low savings among the poor, tests it.",
